                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_1 p_1 i_2 p_2 = i_2 p_2 i_1 p_1.
          %p
            See Definition 3.1 in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Karvonen20"} Karvonen 2020
          %h2#SemiadditiveCategories Semiadditive categories
          %p
            A category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with all finite biproducts is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong semiadditive category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has all finite products and coproducts, that the unique map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to 1
            is an isomorphism (hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has a zero object), and that the canonical maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊔
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1 \sqcup c_2 \to c_1 \times c_2
            defined above are isomorphisms.
          %p
            Amusingly, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to be semiadditive, it actually suffices to assume that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has finite products and coproducts and that there exists
            %em any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural
            family of isomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊔
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1 \sqcup c_2 \cong c_1 \times c_2
            — not necessarily the canonical maps constructed above. A proof can be found in (
            %a{:href => "#Lack09"}> Lack 09
            ).
          %p
            An
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+category"} additive category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+property"} extra property
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#BiproductsImplyEnrichment"} Properties – Biproducts imply enrichment
          %h3#as_cocartesian_objects As (co)cartesian objects
          %p
            The aforementioned result of Lack implies that a semiadditive category is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+object"} cocartesian object
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+categories"} cartesian monoidal categories
            and product-preserving functors. In fact, by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+argument"} Eckmann-Hilton
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            of such a 2-category has to coincide with the one given by the cartesian product of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊔
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1 \sqcup c_2 \cong c_1 \times c_2
          %p
            The exact same argument also shows semiadditive categories are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+objects"} cartesian objects
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocartesian+monoidal+categories"} cocartesian monoidal categories
            and coproduct-preserving functors.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#SemiadditivityAsStructureProperty Semiadditivity as structure/property
          %p
            Given a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+morphisms"} zero morphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            of a chosen
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\psi_{(-),(-)} : (-)\sqcup (-) \stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow} (-)\times(-)
                  \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Lack09"}> Lack 09, proof of theorem 5
              ). If a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              with finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
              carries any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_{(-),(-)}
              from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ⊔
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi ψ
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi c
                                      %mn 1
                                    %mo ,
                                    %mn 0
                                %mo +
                                %msub
                                  %mi ψ
                                  %mrow
                                    %mn 0
                                    %mo ,
                                    %msub
                                      %mi c
                                      %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ⊔
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi r
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi c
                                      %mn 1
                                    %mo ,
                                    %msub
                                      %mi c
                                      %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi ψ
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi c
                                      %mn 1
                                    %mo ,
                                    %msub
                                      %mi c
                                      %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mn 1
                            %mo ×
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array {
                    c_1\sqcup c_2 &amp; \overset{\psi_{c_1, 0} + \psi_{0, c_2}}\rightarrow &amp; c_1 \sqcup c_2 \\
                    \&amp; \searrow^{r_{c_1, c_2}}           &amp; \downarrow^{\psi_{c_1, c_2}} \\
                    \&amp;                        &amp; c_1 \times c_2
                    }
            %p
              commutes for any two object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_2
          %p
            Hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r_{c_1, c_2}
            is an isomorphism so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is semi-additive. See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-canonical+isomorphism"} non-canonical isomorphism
            for more.
          %h3#BiproductsImplyEnrichment Biproducts imply enrichment – Relation to additive categories
          %p
            A semiadditive category is automatically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoids"} commutative monoids
            with the usual
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
          %p
            Given two morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, g: a \to b
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo +
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f + g: a \to b
            be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi a
                  %mo ×
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi a
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi g
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⊔
                  %mi b
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \to a \times a \cong a \oplus a \overset{f \oplus g}{\to} b \oplus b \cong b \sqcup b \to b .
          %p
            One proves that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            is associative and commutative. Of course, the zero morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0: a \to b
            is the usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+morphism"} zero morphism
            given by the zero object:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ≅
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \to 1 \cong 0 \to b .
          %p
            One proves that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is the neutral element for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            and that this matches the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            morphism that we began with in the definition. Note that in addition to a zero object, this construction actually only requires biproducts of an object with itself, i.e. biproducts of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\oplus a
            rather than the more general
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\oplus b
          %p
            If additionally every morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: a \to b
            has an inverse
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -f: a \to b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is enriched over the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ab
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            and is therefore (precisely) an
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+category"} additive category
          %p
            If, on the other hand, the addition of morphisms is idempotent (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo +
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f+f=f
            ), then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is enriched over the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SLat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SLat
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semilattices"} semilattices
            (and is therefore a kind of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-poset"} 2-poset
          %h3#biproducts_as_enriched_cauchy_colimits Biproducts as enriched Cauchy colimits
          %p
            Conversely, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is already known to be enriched over commutative monoids, then a binary biproduct may be defined purely diagrammatically as an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1\oplus c_2
            together with injections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_i:c_i\to c_1\oplus c_2
            and projections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_i:c_1\oplus c_2 \to c_i
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_j n_i = \delta_{i j}
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kronecker+delta"} Kronecker delta
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ⊕
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_1 p_1 + n_2 p_2 = 1_{c_1\oplus c_2}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1\oplus c_2
            a biproduct, and that any binary biproduct must be of this form. Similarly, an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            of such a category is a zero object precisely when
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 0
                      %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_z= 0_z
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+colimit"} Cauchy colimit
            (i.e.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+colimit"} absolute colimit
            %em or
            coproduct in a category enriched over commutative monoids is actually a biproduct.
          %p
            For categories enriched over
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suplattices"} suplattices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⊕
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_1 p_1 + n_2 p_2 = 1_{c_1\oplus c_2}
            replaced by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                          %mi i
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigvee_{i} n_i p_i = 1_{\bigoplus_i c_i}
          %h3#biproducts_from_duals Biproducts from duals
          %p
            The existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+objects"} dual objects
            tends to imply (semi)additivity; see (
            %a{:href => "#Houston06"}> Houston 08
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/14402/semiadditivity-and-dualizability-of-2/21307"} MO discussion
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p Categories with biproducts include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
                of abelian groups. More generally, any
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %li
              %p
                The category of (finitely generated)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+module"} projective modules
                over a given ring.
            %li
              %p
                Any
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
                of a ring, or the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+category"} homotopy category of spectra
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functors"} exact functors
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%28n%29-local+stable+homotopy+theory"} K(n)-local stable homotopy theory
                (
                %a{:href => "#HopkinsLurie14"}> Hopkins-Lurie 14
                )
            %li
              %p
                The category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
                of sets and relations between sets.
            %li
              %p
                The category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SupLat"} SupLat
                of sup-lattices.
            %li
              %p
                Any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
                with products (or coproducts); see
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Houston06"} Houston 06
                %a{:href => "#GarnerSchaeppi15"} Garner-Schaeppi 15
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#Zekic21"} Zekic 21
          %h2#nonexamples Non-examples
          %p
            Some categories possess all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+products"} binary products
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
            but they are not biproducts and the category is thus not a semiadditive category. From above, we know that they are not enriched over the category of commutative monoids.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                of sets and functions, where the product is given by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
                of sets and the coproduct by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
                of sets.
            %li
              %p
                The category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                of topological spaces and continuous functions where the product is again given by the cartesian product and the coproduct by the disjoint union.
            %li
              %p
                The category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
                of groups, where the product is given by the cartesian product and the coproduct by the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+product+of+groups"} free product of groups
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilimit"} bilimit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiadditive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} semiadditive (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+category"} pointed category
          %h2#references References
          %ul
